  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe Pacific cold front has now moved through the CWA as of early\nthis morning. Temperatures have cooled into the 40s and 50s, with\nsome areas already in the 30s. Much of the precipitation has\nexited the CWA, with just a mix of light rain/snow falling over\nthe Rocky Mountain Front. Afternoon temperatures will be quite a\nbit cooler today, with most areas about 20 degrees cooler than\nyesterday. \n\nFor Sunday, it should start out dry, but an upper level\ndisturbance will move northward into Southwest MT by Sunday\nevening, producing a few scattered showers/thunderstorms. \n\nThe flow aloft becomes more westerly for Mon thru Friday of next\nweek. This will allow for mostly dry conditions, along with a nice\nwarming trend. Afternoon temperatures by the middle of next week\ncould average between 4 and 8 degrees above normal.\n\nFor next weekend, the GFS/EC models still disagree a bit, but not\nas much as yesterday. Overall, the GFS is not as cold/aggressive\nwith the precipitation. Additionally, any cool air that moves \ninto the CWA next weekend looks to be short lived, as warm air \nshould move back in rather quickly.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe high wind warning will be cancelled a bit early for areas \neast of Cut Bank this morning.\n\nFor the Cut Bank/Browning areas...the high wind warning will\ncontinue, as winds have been gusting between 60 and 80 mph in this\narea early this morning.\n\nThe precipitation next weekend is not looking excessive at this\ntime, with most areas receiving less than a quarter inch. Brusda",
